   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a license\nto sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises",
    "basePrintNo": "S6316",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6316-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §64, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6316\n\nSPONSOR: ROLISON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a\nlicense to sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide the Silk Factory in the City of\nNewburgh an exemption from the 200-foot rule so they can use their Wash-\nington Street entrance to obtain a liquor license.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n§ 1 adds paragraph (e-15) to subdivision 7 of section 64-a of the alco-\nholic beverage control law. This paragraph outline the metes and bounds\nof the establishment, which are within 200 feet of a church and provide\nan exemption to obtain a liquor license.\n\n§ 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nUnder state law, no restaurant or bar may serve liquor within 200 feet\nof a church or house of worship. Currently, the Silk Factory cannot use\ntheir ashington Street entrance and serve alcohol because they are\nlocated within 200 feet of Sacred Heart Church.\n\nSacred Heart Church has given its approval to the Silk Factory to seek\nthis exemption and utilize their Washington Street entrance. The\nexemption is necessary to increase convenience and allow the establish-\nment to modify their current layout.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
